WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.789 --> 00:00:05.609
LINDSAY: Welcome, everyone, to tonight's on
the record Council on Foreign Relations meeting.

00:00:05.609 --> 00:00:11.179
I am Jim Lindsay, the director of studies
here at the Council on Foreign Relations and

00:00:11.179 --> 00:00:19.360
it is my great pleasure to introduce tonight's
guests of honor, Elizabeth Economy and Michael

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:20.360
Levi.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:26.460
Liz Economy is C.V. Starr senior fellow and
director of Asia Studies here at the Council.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:34.310
Liz has published widely on China and U.S.-China
relations during her time here at CFR.

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:40.300
She is the author of "The River Runs Black:
The Environmental Challenge to China's Future”,

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:44.970
which is a classic in the field and I highly
recommend it to those of you who have not

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:46.870
yet read it.

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:54.300
Besides writing books, Liz is a master of
the short form publication, contributing regularly

00:00:54.300 --> 00:01:00.910
blog posts at the awarding-winning CFR blog
Asia Unbound, which I'm required by contract

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:01.910
to tell you this...

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:02.910
(LAUGHTER)

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:05.880
You can find at CFR.org.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:11.140
Liz is also vice chair of the World Economic
Forum's Global Agenda Council on the Future

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:18.830
of China. And she serves on the board of the
China-U.S. Center for Sustainable Development.

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:24.340
To Liz's right is Michael Levi. Michael is
a David M. Rubenstein Senior Fellow for Energy

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:29.570
and the Environment and he's also director
of the program on Energy Security and Climate

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:32.710
Change here at CFR.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:40.060
Michael's many publications have covered topics
such as arms control, energy security, nuclear

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:42.390
terrorism and climate change.

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:48.221
His book "The Power Surge: Energy, Opportunity,
and the Battle for America's Future," which

00:01:48.221 --> 00:01:56.220
was published last year is a path-breaking
analysis of the energy revolution that's currently

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:59.460
undergoing -- we're undergoing here in the
United States.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:06.939
Like Liz, Michael is also a master of the
short form publication format with his blog

00:02:06.939 --> 00:02:13.890
at CFR Energy, Security and Climate, which
provides cutting-edge analysis, not surprisingly,

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:22.079
on energy, security and climate change matters;
and you can find it, too, on CFR.org.

00:02:22.079 --> 00:02:26.260
But we're not here to talk about their success
in blogging...

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:27.260
(LAUGHTER)

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:31.420
Or about their previous books. We are here
to talk about their new book that they've

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:38.029
written jointly, which I could hold up here,
called "By All Means Necessary: How China's

00:02:38.029 --> 00:02:40.200
Resource Quest is Changing the World."

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:47.700
So, please join me in welcoming Liz and Michael.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.290
(APPLAUSE)

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:55.709
First off, congratulations on completing the
book. Since I got to see it at various stages

00:02:55.709 --> 00:03:01.059
during manuscript form, I know how much time
and effort went into producing it.

00:03:01.059 --> 00:03:06.499
And I note that they say you cannot judge
a book by its cover, but I think the cover

00:03:06.499 --> 00:03:14.209
of the book is quite attractive. But presumably,
the title says something about the book's

00:03:14.209 --> 00:03:15.209
contents.

00:03:15.209 --> 00:03:20.309
So, at the risk of violating the moderator's
protocol, which is never to ask two questions

00:03:20.309 --> 00:03:23.790
at once, I'm going to ask two questions at
once.

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:32.340
First, Liz, why by all means necessary? And
Michael, how exactly is China's resource quest

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:33.340
changing the world?

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:34.549
Liz, you go first.

00:03:34.549 --> 00:03:37.950
ECONOMY: Thank you. And thanks very much,
Jim, for hosting this event. It's a real pleasure

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:39.370
for us to be here.

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:46.510
So, by all means necessary really refers to
all the tools that China brings to the table

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:51.959
when it goes out to secure resources from
abroad. These are tools that in many cases

00:03:51.959 --> 00:03:58.139
other countries and multinationals either
can't or simply don't utilize.

00:03:58.139 --> 00:04:05.140
Let me just talk about three. So, first, right,
when China goes out to get iron ore from Gabon,

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:11.569
from example, it may not simply have one state-owned
enterprise go out to -- to access that resource;

00:04:11.569 --> 00:04:16.320
but rather, Xi Jinping, the president of the
country, will lead a delegation made up of

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:22.950
ministers and state-owned enterprises and
sort of lay out these vast trade, aid, and

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:25.170
investment deals.

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:31.630
A second way is that, in fact, you can see
that China is much more comfortable in striking

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:38.420
backroom deals, right? So, you can find Xi
Jinping or other members of his government

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:44.570
coordinating with other leaders of countries
to strike deals that will bring benefits to

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:45.950
either side.

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:53.550
So, I think that if you look at the way in
which China approaches its deal -- deal-making,

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:58.260
you see that it's a sort of different scenario
in many respects from the way that the United

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:03.000
States of our multinationals are able to go
out doing -- doing things.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:08.140
The third one I'll just -- I'll just mention
briefly is sort of the ability of China to

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:14.050
bring low-cost financing to the table -- low-cost
labor. Here, too, this is something that makes

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:18.290
it very difficult, on occasion, for our multinationals
to compete.

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:24.160
LEVI: So, China's resource quest is changing
the world in a host of different ways. And

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:29.110
we talk about a lot those in the book. It's
changing the world through the impact on the

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:34.520
ground of its investments, in labor, in the
environment.

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:40.020
It's changing the world through the way it
-- its military increasingly thinks about

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:45.170
security for sea lanes and bringing the resources
home. And it's -- it's changing the world

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:50.200
through the impact that its resource relationships
have on how it behaves in the security council

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:52.240
and bilateral relationships.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:58.080
But what surprised us as we worked on the
book and as we worked through this is that

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:03.030
for most of the time, all of those are overshadowed
by something much simpler -- the impact of

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:05.730
China's resource quest through trade.

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:10.441
China is an enormous country. And regardless
of what its strategy is, its sheer size, its

00:06:10.441 --> 00:06:16.790
sheer demand for imports -- it's going to
have wide spread consequences for global markets

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:20.770
and for societies and for security as a result.

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:26.590
China's demand for resources ranging from
oil to iron ore has sent prices up. That has

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:31.240
consequences for the economies of countries
that consume these resources, that produce

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:36.460
them -- regardless of whether China actually
has any direct relationship with those; those

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:41.160
high prices have security spillovers, they
have governance spillovers.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:45.490
So, when you come back to it, there are all
these things happening. We have to pay attention;

00:06:45.490 --> 00:06:46.800
we have to deal with them.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:50.120
But this one dominant piece may not be so
sexy -- may not have all the great stories

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:55.050
with it. But it really is at least one of
the central ways that China is changing the

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:56.050
world.

00:06:56.050 --> 00:06:59.190
LINDSAY: Fair enough. Thank you for painting
the big picture. We can now -- sort of want

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:00.680
to drill down.

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:08.139
I suspect everybody in the room here has a
sense that China is emerging as a global powerhouse

00:07:08.139 --> 00:07:13.360
-- that it's going abroad in search of commodities
and what have you.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:19.430
But I'm not sure, Liz, that we have a really
good sense of where China is sort of focusing

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:22.820
its attentions and -- and sort of what commodities
it's going after.

00:07:22.820 --> 00:07:28.360
So, perhaps you could just sort of paint for
us a picture of where China's -- Chinese firms

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:31.590
are operating, where they're particularly
concentrating in, what kind of commodities

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:33.450
they deal with; that would really help.

00:07:33.450 --> 00:07:37.180
ECONOMY: Well, when you have the second largest
economy in the world that's been growing at

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:40.990
a rate of, you know, roughly, you know, 10
percent per year for almost 20 years until

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:44.400
recently, they're going after everything everywhere.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:53.820
So, you know, we look in the book at minerals;
we look at oil and gas; we look at water and

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:57.320
we look at -- minerals, oil and gas, water
and...

00:07:57.320 --> 00:07:58.320
LEVI: Land or food.

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:00.930
ECONOMY: Land, thank you -- and land or food.

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:04.760
And here, we see that China is everywhere.
It's in Africa, it's in Southeast Asia, it's

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:08.600
in Latin America and it's, you know, also
in advanced industrialized countries like

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:14.000
the United States, Canada and Australia; and
we look at those separately from sort of the

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:15.440
developing world.

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:20.280
So, it's not particularly focused, you know,
in one region. I mean, I think there's been

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:25.481
an enormous amount of attention paid to China's
engagement in Africa, for example. But we've

00:08:25.481 --> 00:08:29.530
found that China's very deeply engaged, for
example, in Latin America, as well -- and

00:08:29.530 --> 00:08:31.980
certainly in its own backyard in Southeast
Asia.

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:35.940
LINDSAY: Obviously Michael, you know, one
of the consequences of China going abroad

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:42.149
in search of commodities is that it's given
a boost to commodity prices.

00:08:42.149 --> 00:08:47.600
And I think, you know, when -- when sort of
the Chinese economy really began to rev up,

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:52.889
you began to hear a lot of stories about how
in essence the Chinese are going to remake

00:08:52.889 --> 00:08:57.740
these markets -- we're going to move away
from a market-based approach to the distribution

00:08:57.740 --> 00:09:02.499
of commodities to its variously called state
capitalism where they would lock up commodities

00:09:02.499 --> 00:09:08.490
under long-term contracts and it would really
fundamentally change the game. Is that what

00:09:08.490 --> 00:09:09.999
you see happening?

00:09:09.999 --> 00:09:15.879
LEVI: It's not what I see happening in most
cases. And more than that -- and I think this

00:09:15.879 --> 00:09:20.391
struck us both as surprising when we're working
on the book -- there are occasions where China

00:09:20.391 --> 00:09:24.120
not intentionally is actually leading the
world to a more market-based approach.

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:29.699
So, the piece you hear a lot is that China
is locking up oil and is going to move us

00:09:29.699 --> 00:09:34.180
away from a global market that sort of separates
economics and politics to one where they're

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:35.730
much more intimately tied.

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:42.700
But what you find is even as China acquires
more resources -- gets more equity oil investment,

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:47.579
it's still trading most of the product on
the world market. It's still trying to get

00:09:47.579 --> 00:09:51.490
a high price or as high a price as it can
for it.

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:54.540
And so, it turns out that the -- you know,
the world economy -- China may be big, but

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:58.660
the world economy is pretty big, too. It's
a powerful system. It's pretty resilient.

00:09:58.660 --> 00:10:04.110
The -- the surprising case -- and I never
expected to find iron ore interesting -- but

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:09.209
this is the surprising case, is iron ore.
Iron ore markets are not like the markets

00:10:09.209 --> 00:10:13.621
we think of where everyone goes and says and
sort of yells their buy and sell prices and

00:10:13.621 --> 00:10:17.050
you come efficiently to a nice in-between.

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:22.230
It used to be one dominated by three big players,
by a handful of big sellers, a handful of

00:10:22.230 --> 00:10:26.569
big consumers; they would get together and
do very structured negotiations every year,

00:10:26.569 --> 00:10:29.160
come out with a price, everyone would pay
it. And then you'd come back the next year

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:31.160
and you'd do it again.

00:10:31.160 --> 00:10:36.990
And when China entered this as a big buyer,
it didn't seek to upend it. But you had a

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:42.950
host of small Chinese steel mills, and they
all went out and they all wanted to get their

00:10:42.950 --> 00:10:46.810
own deal; they didn't want to play that game.
And the Chinese government, for all its power,

00:10:46.810 --> 00:10:49.630
wasn't able to get them to do what it wanted.

00:10:49.630 --> 00:10:55.360
All those small players doing their own deals
created an opportunity for a real market to

00:10:55.360 --> 00:11:00.230
emerge. And then a few other pieces catalyzed
action and over the course of a couple years,

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:03.959
the world iron ore market was transformed
from this structure -- a pretty political

00:11:03.959 --> 00:11:06.420
one -- to a much more market-based one.

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:11.709
So, a surprising outcome; but again, I think
a sort of example where whatever impact China

00:11:11.709 --> 00:11:15.329
has -- even if it's not the one you expect
-- because it's so big, it's going to have

00:11:15.329 --> 00:11:16.329
consequences.

00:11:16.329 --> 00:11:21.459
LINDSAY: OK. You know, the -- the notion of
Chinese companies going abroad probably shouldn't

00:11:21.459 --> 00:11:23.230
have been surprising to people.

00:11:23.230 --> 00:11:28.569
After all, U.S. companies, when the United
States became economically powerful, went

00:11:28.569 --> 00:11:34.779
abroad as well. But typically, when U.S. companies
go abroad, they're not doing the bidding of

00:11:34.779 --> 00:11:35.959
Washington D.C.

00:11:35.959 --> 00:11:41.149
And one of the sort of flip concerns of the
whole China is going to recreate a mercantilist

00:11:41.149 --> 00:11:47.970
economy is that Chinese firms being state-owned
enterprises -- SOEs is the in-house acronym

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:54.269
we use for them -- go abroad that they will
be doing the bidding of Beijing; that the

00:11:54.269 --> 00:11:59.679
idea isn't necessarily to advance economic,
per se, but broader political interest.

00:11:59.679 --> 00:12:01.420
Is this what you've found in the book, Liz?

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:05.639
ECONOMY: It's a -- it's a mix, really. And
here, too, as Mike said, it was a little bit

00:12:05.639 --> 00:12:07.019
of a surprise, I think.

00:12:07.019 --> 00:12:14.019
First off, yes, the Chinese government does
want to direct companies to certain resources

00:12:14.019 --> 00:12:19.319
and the National Development and Reform Commission
sort of lays out what resources it needs and

00:12:19.319 --> 00:12:23.559
it does put together these big plans for individual
countries.

00:12:23.559 --> 00:12:28.869
But then it's up to the companies to decide
to participate in those plans. And in many

00:12:28.869 --> 00:12:33.239
instances, we've found, you know, that investment
would be announced by the Chinese government

00:12:33.239 --> 00:12:37.910
-- for example, in Brazil, there's been something
like $70 billion worth of investment announced

00:12:37.910 --> 00:12:42.060
since 2007, but the realized investment is
about a third of that.

00:12:42.060 --> 00:12:46.209
Now, part of that has to do with, you know,
challenges that any company would face doing

00:12:46.209 --> 00:12:50.019
business in Brazil. But another part of it
has to do with the fact that, you know, Chinese

00:12:50.019 --> 00:12:53.949
companies sometimes simply don't want to play
the way that the Chinese state is telling

00:12:53.949 --> 00:12:55.069
them to.

00:12:55.069 --> 00:12:59.890
The second thing that, you know, sort of is
a little bit different than what we had anticipated

00:12:59.890 --> 00:13:04.990
in terms of the sort of overall control of
the Chinese government in the sort of resource

00:13:04.990 --> 00:13:09.259
quest is that there are many more small actors.

00:13:09.259 --> 00:13:13.740
So, it does vary by sector and -- you know,
oil and gas, they tend to be, you know, fewer

00:13:13.740 --> 00:13:18.639
and -- and major players. But in the mining
sector, for example, you know, only a third

00:13:18.639 --> 00:13:24.240
of the companies are state-owned enterprises
and, you know, the rest two-thirds are -- are

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:26.249
sort of smaller scale firms.

00:13:26.249 --> 00:13:31.450
And you see them going out, right, without
any sort of permission or direction of Beijing.

00:13:31.450 --> 00:13:35.749
And in many cases, they cause a lot of problems
for the Chinese government.

00:13:35.749 --> 00:13:42.459
For example, in Ghana we saw last July that,
you know, the Ghanaian government kicked out,

00:13:42.459 --> 00:13:48.199
you know, 4,500, 5,000 Chinese miners. They
were illegal miners; they were, you know,

00:13:48.199 --> 00:13:50.149
their environmental practices were poor.

00:13:50.149 --> 00:13:55.569
And yes, the Chinese government was upset
and there's a little bit of retaliation going

00:13:55.569 --> 00:14:00.170
on. But in point of fact, it certainly wasn't
the Chinese government telling those miners

00:14:00.170 --> 00:14:04.050
to go out and secure gold from Ghana. Thanks.

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:10.889
LINDSAY: Let's talk a little bit about impact,
OK? Because obviously, with Chinese companies

00:14:10.889 --> 00:14:17.519
going out, they were a big factor behind boom
in commodities -- we saw over the last decade.

00:14:17.519 --> 00:14:20.959
But now, when I open the pages of the Wall
Street Journal, what I keep reading about

00:14:20.959 --> 00:14:26.519
is that commodity boom is coming to an end.
The great growth that Brazil received, for

00:14:26.519 --> 00:14:32.619
example, because of a very sort of vibrant
Chinese market seems to have cooled down.

00:14:32.619 --> 00:14:38.199
Is -- is something fundamentally changing
in -- in China's quest for commodities, Michael?

00:14:38.199 --> 00:14:40.660
Is this a leveling out? Is it cyclical? (Inaudible)...

00:14:40.660 --> 00:14:42.410
LEVI: I think the world is catching up.

00:14:42.410 --> 00:14:43.410
LINDSAY: OK.

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:48.209
LEVI: And look, you have to look at individual
commodities one by one. But when we were writing

00:14:48.209 --> 00:14:51.069
the book, we decided to start, instead of
with predicting the future, by looking at

00:14:51.069 --> 00:14:57.439
the past -- and asked why is it that rapidly
growing Chinese demand for commodities drove

00:14:57.439 --> 00:15:01.920
up prices so much in so many commodities over
the last decade?

00:15:01.920 --> 00:15:05.139
Because the reality is, there are a lot of
things in the world where demand increases

00:15:05.139 --> 00:15:10.199
rapidly and the price stays roughly the same.
My iPad doesn't cost more than it would have

00:15:10.199 --> 00:15:12.949
a few years ago, but demand has gone up.

00:15:12.949 --> 00:15:18.589
And -- and what you find in so many of these
sectors is that the lead time for development

00:15:18.589 --> 00:15:24.879
is enormous. And to compound that, a lot of
them were expecting very limited growth and

00:15:24.879 --> 00:15:28.889
so, they have to -- their sectors really had
to shift gears quickly in the places where

00:15:28.889 --> 00:15:32.779
either the sector was more responsive or able
to be more responsive or they were already

00:15:32.779 --> 00:15:36.160
expecting growth for a lot of other reasons
-- aluminum is an example; you didn't see

00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:38.619
the same kinds of price run-ups.

00:15:38.619 --> 00:15:44.910
But now we are seeing these areas catching
up. There has been a lot more exploration;

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:49.170
there is money put in, mines that take time
to develop and technological progress.

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:52.410
And we're seeing this in the United States
right now. We would not be having an American

00:15:52.410 --> 00:15:58.429
oil and gas boom, ironically, if it were not
for China's global resource quest. The prices

00:15:58.429 --> 00:16:04.089
would not have been there to prompt that response
that's happening here that's moderating prices

00:16:04.089 --> 00:16:06.230
in turn, had we not had that initial spark.

00:16:06.230 --> 00:16:10.999
LINDSAY: So, higher prices encourage producers
to invest to bring more production online,

00:16:10.999 --> 00:16:12.179
which drives prices back down.

00:16:12.179 --> 00:16:13.179
ECONOMY: Yes.

00:16:13.179 --> 00:16:14.899
LEVI: Which stops them from rising too much
more...

00:16:14.899 --> 00:16:15.899
LINDSAY: Much more...

00:16:15.899 --> 00:16:18.569
LEVI: Further. Look, we're not going to -- to
sit here and predict exactly how the future

00:16:18.569 --> 00:16:20.819
will turn out and you can out-surprise us.

00:16:20.819 --> 00:16:26.720
But if I had to pick a surprise now, it would
be on the downside, because when -- you know,

00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:29.660
the thing that can have the biggest impact
on prices is when the world turns out in a

00:16:29.660 --> 00:16:31.939
way differently from what you expect.

00:16:31.939 --> 00:16:37.939
We now expect continued massive growth in
commodities demand. So, if you want a surprise,

00:16:37.939 --> 00:16:39.420
you should look for a surprise on the downside.

00:16:39.420 --> 00:16:45.600
LINDSAY: OK. I -- let -- question on another
aspect of impact, Liz, which is the impact

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:51.439
of China's resource quest abroad on environmental
practices in other countries.

00:16:51.439 --> 00:16:56.449
Obviously, you turn the TV on and you -- and
you have written quite extensively before

00:16:56.449 --> 00:17:03.489
it was a lead story in the news about what
Chinese growth is doing to the Chinese economy.

00:17:03.489 --> 00:17:10.610
Is China doing abroad the things we sort of
see happening in places like Shanghai and

00:17:10.610 --> 00:17:11.610
Beijing?

00:17:11.610 --> 00:17:16.440
ECONOMY: Yes. And so, even I think more broadly
than simply on the environment, when you look

00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:21.660
at any issue of sort of governance, whether
it's the environment or whether it's sort

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:27.980
of corruption issues or whether it's labor
issues, we found that China at home is really

00:17:27.980 --> 00:17:32.261
China abroad. So, if you want understand China's
impact abroad, you just look at whatever it

00:17:32.261 --> 00:17:33.510
is that they're doing at home.

00:17:33.510 --> 00:17:37.520
So, the fact that a lot of Chinese companies,
for example, don't undertake environmental

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:43.140
impact assessments at home means that when
they're in Zambia, they're pretty much also

00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:48.190
not undertaking environmental impact assessments
-- if they can get away with it.

00:17:48.190 --> 00:17:52.780
And different states have different capacities.
So, I think one of the other findings we had

00:17:52.780 --> 00:17:56.950
was that the international systems can be
a constraining factor -- so that the way that,

00:17:56.950 --> 00:18:01.890
you know, China can operate in, you know,
Zambia is quite different from the way it,

00:18:01.890 --> 00:18:05.670
you know, can operate in Brazil -- even though
it may try to do the exact same thing.

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:10.730
But -- but certainly, there has been enormous
environmental impact from Chinese companies

00:18:10.730 --> 00:18:16.140
going abroad. And it's a source of significant
tension in communities wherever you look -- whether

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:20.960
it's Latin America, Africa or Southeast Asia;
there's a lot of concern about what China

00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:24.800
is doing -- what its enterprises are doing,
in terms of the environment.

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:30.660
LINDSAY: At this point, I'd like to bring
audience members into our conversation. I

00:18:30.660 --> 00:18:35.270
have to ask people -- if you would like to
be called on, raise your hand -- I ask that

00:18:35.270 --> 00:18:40.630
you wait a moment 'til we bring the microphone
over. Please speak directly into the microphone

00:18:40.630 --> 00:18:44.100
and stand, state your name and affiliation.

00:18:44.100 --> 00:18:52.370
I'm going to go all the way in the back -- we're
-- start in the back and bounce around.

00:18:52.370 --> 00:18:53.370
Oh, OK -- I see.

00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:54.370
(LAUGHTER)

00:18:54.370 --> 00:18:55.370
ECONOMY: She was just waving at us up here.
Hi.

00:18:55.370 --> 00:18:58.040
LINDSAY: Oh, OK. Well, do you want to wave
back and then move on? I'll start at the front

00:18:58.040 --> 00:19:02.150
then, sir -- Dexter (ph), if you could.

00:19:02.150 --> 00:19:07.170
QUESTION: Real alarm -- no. Liz, I wanted
to ask you on the financing side rather than

00:19:07.170 --> 00:19:12.310
the resourcing side, how much of this would
or could happen without China's sovereign

00:19:12.310 --> 00:19:13.310
wealth funds?

00:19:13.310 --> 00:19:19.480
I mean, is all of this supported by CIC and
sovereign wealth funds or none of it? I don't

00:19:19.480 --> 00:19:21.000
-- I have no idea.

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:24.500
ECONOMY: No, I think -- Jim Mann (ph), by
the way, since you didn't identify yourself.

00:19:24.500 --> 00:19:25.500
(LAUGHTER)

00:19:25.500 --> 00:19:30.280
But no, in fact, China's sovereign -- CIC
didn't really get into the natural resource

00:19:30.280 --> 00:19:36.770
business until maybe five or six years ago;
and then it was directed by the Chinese -- by

00:19:36.770 --> 00:19:38.780
Beijing to do so.

00:19:38.780 --> 00:19:44.250
And so, a lot of what we've seen taking place
over the past 10 to 15 years is not related

00:19:44.250 --> 00:19:48.150
to CIC investment. You know, the majority
of the finances come through X China, Exim

00:19:48.150 --> 00:19:52.511
Bank or the China Development Bank -- those
are really the powerhouse players when it

00:19:52.511 --> 00:19:58.430
comes to the Chinese investment in the resources.

00:19:58.430 --> 00:20:00.540
LINDSAY: Fair enough. Right here.

00:20:00.540 --> 00:20:04.950
QUESTION: Priscilla Clapp. I'm retired from
the State Department.

00:20:04.950 --> 00:20:11.990
In the areas in which I'm concerned, China
is having to change its ways on environment

00:20:11.990 --> 00:20:21.750
and social policies, importing labor, training
local labor. Do you see that happening across

00:20:21.750 --> 00:20:24.790
the board, or is it just special for their
neighbors?

00:20:24.790 --> 00:20:32.320
ECONOMY: So, Burma Myanmar, for example, I
think there is a process of learning going

00:20:32.320 --> 00:20:37.760
on. And in terms of China's general corporate
social responsibility practices, there is

00:20:37.760 --> 00:20:42.230
pressure coming from the bottom up, from the
outside in and from the top down.

00:20:42.230 --> 00:20:48.870
And so, certainly there are NGOs, for example,
as you know, going into Burma Myanmar from

00:20:48.870 --> 00:20:54.820
China and talking to local communities and
going back and saying to the Chinese companies

00:20:54.820 --> 00:20:57.650
and to Beijing, you know, "We really messed
up here," right?

00:20:57.650 --> 00:20:58.890
QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE) Chinese NGOs?

00:20:58.890 --> 00:21:02.990
ECONOMY: Chinese NGOs, like Green Watershed,
for example. You know, sort of undertaking

00:21:02.990 --> 00:21:06.270
studies and saying, you know, "You need to
consult with local communities," right? You

00:21:06.270 --> 00:21:10.230
can't simply deal with the officials, right?
You need to engage the local population.

00:21:10.230 --> 00:21:15.050
So, there are -- there are people within China,
you know, at the grassroots level, who are

00:21:15.050 --> 00:21:18.110
-- I think are bringing news back about what
needs to be done.

00:21:18.110 --> 00:21:22.940
You know, we found that places like Peru,
for example, that in the first Chinese investment

00:21:22.940 --> 00:21:30.240
back in the mid-1990s and the Shougang Mining
Company -- you know, terrible, terrible practices,

00:21:30.240 --> 00:21:32.280
terrible reputation.

00:21:32.280 --> 00:21:37.300
But over time, you know, as six, seven, you
know, eight more mining companies have come

00:21:37.300 --> 00:21:42.020
from China, they're learning from Shougang's
negative experience. And they're taking advantage

00:21:42.020 --> 00:21:46.610
of an infrastructure in Peru that's been developed
by western companies, right, and hiring western

00:21:46.610 --> 00:21:52.670
consultants or even taking on a local, you
know, CEO to manage things -- to deal with

00:21:52.670 --> 00:21:55.800
community relations and again, to try to do
things differently.

00:21:55.800 --> 00:22:02.190
And finally, I would say -- and I mentioned
the top down. That really is Beijing recognizing,

00:22:02.190 --> 00:22:07.720
I think, that its companies are causing a
lot of problems for their reputation -- and

00:22:07.720 --> 00:22:12.790
the Foreign Ministry certainly feels under
enormous stress because of what the Chinese

00:22:12.790 --> 00:22:16.110
companies have been doing, especially in places
like Africa.

00:22:16.110 --> 00:22:19.213
And so, they're putting in place a lot of
regulations. I mean, the -- you know, the

00:22:19.213 --> 00:22:24.370
Shenzhen Stock Exchange has a set of regulations
dealing with companies who want to go public.

00:22:24.370 --> 00:22:29.150
You know, you can't have some sort of environmental
black mark against you, right? Exim Bank -- we're

00:22:29.150 --> 00:22:32.850
not going to loan you money unless you've
done an environmental impact assessment.

00:22:32.850 --> 00:22:37.350
But as, you know, we mentioned earlier, China
at home is China abroad. And to the extent

00:22:37.350 --> 00:22:42.130
that any environmental regulation or -- or,
you know, stricture is somehow not being enforced

00:22:42.130 --> 00:22:46.680
on the home front -- it's still not being
enforced necessarily abroad. I would say that

00:22:46.680 --> 00:22:48.100
enforcement is spotty.

00:22:48.100 --> 00:22:52.870
So, corporate social responsibility -- those
things are developing in China and they're

00:22:52.870 --> 00:22:57.990
developing in terms of Chinese practices abroad.
But I would say we're still at a kind of nascent

00:22:57.990 --> 00:22:58.990
stage.

00:22:58.990 --> 00:23:01.730
LINDSAY: We're going to go over here. Sir?

00:23:01.730 --> 00:23:06.120
QUESTION: I'm James Turner from the Daniel
Alexander Payne CDC.

00:23:06.120 --> 00:23:09.620
You mentioned that water and food were two
of the other things that you were looking

00:23:09.620 --> 00:23:13.420
at. Can you speak a little bit more about
those, particularly the impact on the countries

00:23:13.420 --> 00:23:19.450
exporting to China and the human impact in
the potential for conflict?

00:23:19.450 --> 00:23:26.990
ECONOMY: Sure. So, in terms of water, it was
a different kind of investigation that we

00:23:26.990 --> 00:23:31.600
did, because we were looking at China's control
of water within its -- within its boundaries

00:23:31.600 --> 00:23:33.640
and how it affects other countries.

00:23:33.640 --> 00:23:37.870
So, you know, looking at the impact of what
China does with the Ili River, you know, within

00:23:37.870 --> 00:23:42.260
China going up into Kazakhstan or, you know,
the Yarlung Tsangpo and how it flows into

00:23:42.260 --> 00:23:43.260
the Bramahputra.

00:23:43.260 --> 00:23:47.060
But just -- so, it's a little bit different
and we can talk about that. But the land issue

00:23:47.060 --> 00:23:51.100
is one that sort of mirrors the other ones
a little more closely.

00:23:51.100 --> 00:23:56.910
And here, too, one of the surprising things
we found was that China's actually not the

00:23:56.910 --> 00:24:02.250
largest sort of overseas investor, right,
in land -- because what you read, of course,

00:24:02.250 --> 00:24:05.490
is that, you know, China's some kind of, you
know, cookie monster, right? Just out there

00:24:05.490 --> 00:24:06.810
gobbling up...

00:24:06.810 --> 00:24:07.810
(LAUGHTER)

00:24:07.810 --> 00:24:11.380
All the different land. But in point of fact,
the largest overseas investors are first Canada,

00:24:11.380 --> 00:24:16.390
then the United States and then China. So,
I think that helps give it a little bit of

00:24:16.390 --> 00:24:17.550
perspective.

00:24:17.550 --> 00:24:22.340
But I think there is a different kind of concern
when the Chinese companies go out and invest

00:24:22.340 --> 00:24:26.771
in land, and we saw it -- we've seen it in
Brazil and we've seen it in Kazakhstan. There

00:24:26.771 --> 00:24:33.540
is this sense that these state-owned enterprises
are coming to steal the land somehow, right?

00:24:33.540 --> 00:24:40.370
And there's just a -- a different nationalism
that arises that's a little bit stronger than

00:24:40.370 --> 00:24:45.060
you see when it's a U.S. company or some other
kinds of companies going out.

00:24:45.060 --> 00:24:50.870
And so, a number of countries like Brazil,
like Argentina put in place laws, right at

00:24:50.870 --> 00:24:54.860
the time when the Chinese were really going
out, that they said were not in fact directed

00:24:54.860 --> 00:25:00.610
against the Chinese, but pretty clearly had
a significant China component to them to limit

00:25:00.610 --> 00:25:03.900
the amount of outside investment in their
land.

00:25:03.900 --> 00:25:09.420
So, there's a lot of concern out there about
the nature of Chinese investment that perhaps

00:25:09.420 --> 00:25:15.810
didn't exist quite as much for the earlier
entrants like the United States and Canada.

00:25:15.810 --> 00:25:19.250
LINDSAY: Sir?

00:25:19.250 --> 00:25:26.290
QUESTION: Thank you. My name is Roland Amore.
I'm with Oppenheimer.

00:25:26.290 --> 00:25:33.250
I have an observation and a question. As you
know, the world commodities are transacted

00:25:33.250 --> 00:25:37.810
in dollars -- U.S. dollars. And I think if
you look back over the last seven years or

00:25:37.810 --> 00:25:43.620
eight years with the increased demand, it
wasn't as great, I think, as the prices suggest;

00:25:43.620 --> 00:25:46.120
you had Europe faltering, you had the U.S.

00:25:46.120 --> 00:25:53.330
And I think there's a good argument that could
be made that all the loose monetary policy

00:25:53.330 --> 00:25:57.690
has actually exported a little inflation around
the world in the form of commodities. And

00:25:57.690 --> 00:26:01.550
but that brings me to my question -- and that's
the one of currency.

00:26:01.550 --> 00:26:08.130
As the official reserve currency of the world,
all the commodities are certainly traded in

00:26:08.130 --> 00:26:14.440
-- in dollars. As China gets bigger -- and
they're making noises now about a change of

00:26:14.440 --> 00:26:18.250
currency. They -- they can't be number one
unless they're the official world reserve

00:26:18.250 --> 00:26:21.910
currency, or there's third party currency
-- a basket of commodities.

00:26:21.910 --> 00:26:28.350
So, how does currency play into your observations
and your research?

00:26:28.350 --> 00:26:29.441
LEVI: So, it's a...

00:26:29.441 --> 00:26:30.441
LINDSAY: Want to take a crack at that, Mike?

00:26:30.441 --> 00:26:38.200
LEVI: Yeah. So, it's a great question. Over
and over, throughout the research, whether

00:26:38.200 --> 00:26:43.470
it's in this particular question or elsewhere,
what you find is the resource demand has a

00:26:43.470 --> 00:26:48.230
very big impact. But for it to have these
wider consequences, a whole bunch of other

00:26:48.230 --> 00:26:49.810
pieces have to come into place.

00:26:49.810 --> 00:26:54.870
And because the resource part is sort of out
ahead, you don't see some of those following

00:26:54.870 --> 00:26:59.030
consequences. And I think when you look at
the sort of -- the shift in the use of the

00:26:59.030 --> 00:27:02.290
Chinese currency, this is one of those.

00:27:02.290 --> 00:27:09.220
So, could China insist on its resource trade
being transacted and you want -- well, it's

00:27:09.220 --> 00:27:13.950
tough. If it has nicely balanced bilateral
trade with a country and a lot of leverage

00:27:13.950 --> 00:27:18.440
over it, then maybe it can and they'll be
confident that they can, you know, buy various

00:27:18.440 --> 00:27:20.590
Chinese products and exchange.

00:27:20.590 --> 00:27:27.250
But if it isn't, then you need things like
fundamentally different access to Chinese

00:27:27.250 --> 00:27:31.310
markets, different regulation, different legal
approach so that people feel like they can

00:27:31.310 --> 00:27:35.780
actually use Chinese currency as a store value,
not just as a technical way of going back

00:27:35.780 --> 00:27:39.790
right away and buying the thing you were -- you
were planning to buy essentially as a -- sort

00:27:39.790 --> 00:27:43.040
of a -- a very thin mask for a barter system.

00:27:43.040 --> 00:27:47.890
So, while the resource piece I think drives
one part of it, you have to be looking for

00:27:47.890 --> 00:27:51.540
the other pieces to come into place. And -- and
those are going to move much more slowly.

00:27:51.540 --> 00:27:53.260
Those require decisions by China.

00:27:53.260 --> 00:27:57.790
It's not just a matter of Chinese growth creating
demand -- it's a matter of very deliberate

00:27:57.790 --> 00:27:58.980
transformation.

00:27:58.980 --> 00:28:02.540
LINDSAY: Yes, ma'am?

00:28:02.540 --> 00:28:07.500
QUESTION: Paula Stern (ph).

00:28:07.500 --> 00:28:14.980
You talked about iron ore and I'd like to
talk about oil. And particular, in this world

00:28:14.980 --> 00:28:28.240
where we've had OPEC as a cartel -- how China
plays in that world, both -- you know, more

00:28:28.240 --> 00:28:32.370
importantly to me, going forward.

00:28:32.370 --> 00:28:40.350
Demand is obviously there. But it seems to
me that unlike -- well, it's similar from

00:28:40.350 --> 00:28:49.060
your iron ore situation in the sense that
you really do have a -- not a market operating

00:28:49.060 --> 00:28:54.200
that China is trying to participate in. Can
you talk a little bit about that?

00:28:54.200 --> 00:28:59.900
LEVI: So, I -- I've never been sure whether
OPEC is hugely influential or mostly paper

00:28:59.900 --> 00:29:01.660
tiger.

00:29:01.660 --> 00:29:06.320
But the -- but several individual members
of OPEC are clearly influential, whether collectively

00:29:06.320 --> 00:29:14.140
or not, by restraining production and driving
up prices. And China's demand for oil has

00:29:14.140 --> 00:29:19.690
been critical over the last decade in allowing
them to do that.

00:29:19.690 --> 00:29:24.100
If you have several countries that want to
restrain their production in order to send

00:29:24.100 --> 00:29:30.980
up prices, it is much easier to do that when
world demand for oil is rising rapidly. It

00:29:30.980 --> 00:29:38.390
is much easier to divide a growing or even
stagnant pie than to divide a shrinking pie.

00:29:38.390 --> 00:29:44.590
And what China provided over the last decade
or so was a growing pie. And that allowed

00:29:44.590 --> 00:29:49.750
countries to restrain their production, make
more money producing less than they otherwise

00:29:49.750 --> 00:29:51.560
would have.

00:29:51.560 --> 00:29:55.781
Had Chinese demand not been growing, we would've
had a repeat, essentially, of the 1980s where

00:29:55.781 --> 00:30:00.900
OPEC countries were not able to be restrained.
So -- so, China is really a critical catalyst

00:30:00.900 --> 00:30:01.900
there.

00:30:01.900 --> 00:30:04.990
I'll give you just one -- one quick historical
point. If you look back at the projections

00:30:04.990 --> 00:30:12.670
made by various companies or by international
organizations circa 2000 for the world oil

00:30:12.670 --> 00:30:17.150
market, the thing you expect based on the
sort of popular discussions is to see them

00:30:17.150 --> 00:30:19.490
massively lowballing Chinese demand.

00:30:19.490 --> 00:30:24.850
In truth, though, they don't. They greatly
overestimate how much the big old producers

00:30:24.850 --> 00:30:31.450
will be providing. And because they overestimate
it, they get prices wrong. That I think is

00:30:31.450 --> 00:30:35.370
at the core here -- is those two pieces working
together to drive up prices.

00:30:35.370 --> 00:30:43.210
Now, you ask about the future. We now have
supply coming from all sorts of other places,

00:30:43.210 --> 00:30:48.110
so it's not just countries that strategically
restrain it. And you have economic weakness

00:30:48.110 --> 00:30:52.400
around the world and perhaps not as much oil
demand from China as people expected.

00:30:52.400 --> 00:30:57.530
That starts to change the equation, and it's
why I'm skeptical that you'll see a repeat

00:30:57.530 --> 00:31:02.390
of the last decade's run-up over the next
decade.

00:31:02.390 --> 00:31:07.220
LINDSAY: OK. Sir?

00:31:07.220 --> 00:31:13.130
QUESTION: Hi. I'm Sean Barnett from the Institute
for Defense Analyses.

00:31:13.130 --> 00:31:17.180
And I had a question about what impact do
you see China having with all that they're

00:31:17.180 --> 00:31:23.820
doing around the world on the ability of other
nations to obtain scarce resources -- scare

00:31:23.820 --> 00:31:25.250
commodities?

00:31:25.250 --> 00:31:31.830
ECONOMY: So, your question is basically is
China in some way limiting the ability of

00:31:31.830 --> 00:31:34.870
others to -- to access resources?

00:31:34.870 --> 00:31:39.480
QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE) Right, right. Or perhaps
to drive price up to the point where it's

00:31:39.480 --> 00:31:40.940
-- it's (inaudible).

00:31:40.940 --> 00:31:46.640
ECONOMY: Well, I think, you know, it -- within
our book, what we've seen is really that,

00:31:46.640 --> 00:31:52.460
you know, Chinese are enormously competitive.
And to the extent that, you know, their practices

00:31:52.460 --> 00:31:57.870
as I mentioned as sort of backroom practices
can kind of shut out companies. I think that's

00:31:57.870 --> 00:31:59.080
problematic, right?

00:31:59.080 --> 00:32:03.620
They don't really have the same kind of Foreign
Corrupt Practices Act that we do. They have

00:32:03.620 --> 00:32:08.710
something that's along those lines, but there's
actually no monitoring or enforcement capacity

00:32:08.710 --> 00:32:10.310
associated with it.

00:32:10.310 --> 00:32:15.300
So, I think they do provide a number of challenges.
You know, again, those sort of -- those tools

00:32:15.300 --> 00:32:19.259
that I mentioned at the beginning -- the low-cost
financing, the low-cost labor -- you know,

00:32:19.259 --> 00:32:24.640
being able to, you know, export, you know,
a hundred thousand or more, really -- a hundred

00:32:24.640 --> 00:32:29.280
thousand to a million Chinese workers, you
know, to undertake these infrastructure projects

00:32:29.280 --> 00:32:30.820
that go along with the mining projects.

00:32:30.820 --> 00:32:36.590
I mean, I think that makes it challenging
for companies to compete. But I think as Mike

00:32:36.590 --> 00:32:41.460
was describing, and in terms of the price
rises -- you know, the rise in commodity prices,

00:32:41.460 --> 00:32:45.130
you know, it really depends on the market;
really depends on which commodity you're looking

00:32:45.130 --> 00:32:46.170
at.

00:32:46.170 --> 00:32:54.309
And in some, such as, you know, food stuffs
-- grain, other developing countries are contributing

00:32:54.309 --> 00:32:59.290
to price rises. And so, there tends -- there
tends to be this, you know, desire somehow

00:32:59.290 --> 00:33:04.090
or because China occupies so much space in
the media, you know, not to recognize that

00:33:04.090 --> 00:33:05.640
there are other actors out there.

00:33:05.640 --> 00:33:11.520
You know, India, for example, where we look
and see China in Africa and -- and other places

00:33:11.520 --> 00:33:15.990
sometimes maybe not doing exactly what I think
-- India's often right side-by-side. So, I

00:33:15.990 --> 00:33:19.730
think our book does focus on China, but it's
important to understand there are other actors

00:33:19.730 --> 00:33:23.080
out there, as well.

00:33:23.080 --> 00:33:26.150
LINDSAY: Julia? Yep.

00:33:26.150 --> 00:33:28.190
QUESTION: Congratulations.

00:33:28.190 --> 00:33:31.260
LINDSAY: Introduce yourself.

00:33:31.260 --> 00:33:35.630
QUESTION: Oh, Julia Sweig with the Council
on Foreign Relations.

00:33:35.630 --> 00:33:42.750
And I think about Brazil a lot. Both of you
have been there as part of researching this

00:33:42.750 --> 00:33:45.650
book, but I'm not going to ask a Brazil question.

00:33:45.650 --> 00:33:51.580
I want to ask a broader question, which is
you mentioned the downside for Chinese foreign

00:33:51.580 --> 00:34:00.620
policy of having companies playing a little
bit too ugly in fields of afar in Africa and

00:34:00.620 --> 00:34:05.350
that having consequences that are now being
felt back in Beijing.

00:34:05.350 --> 00:34:10.930
So, a downside on the environmental practices
-- but what about the upside? Could you give

00:34:10.930 --> 00:34:17.600
a couple of examples where China as a state
and Chinese foreign policy experiences the

00:34:17.600 --> 00:34:23.950
upside of having such market power in different
regions of the world? And you can include

00:34:23.950 --> 00:34:26.730
Latin America or Brazil, if you'd like. Thank
you.

00:34:26.730 --> 00:34:27.730
(LAUGHTER)

00:34:27.730 --> 00:34:28.730
LINDSAY: Any upside, Michael?

00:34:28.730 --> 00:34:29.730
QUESTION (?): OK.

00:34:29.730 --> 00:34:30.730
(LAUGHTER)

00:34:30.730 --> 00:34:31.730
LEVI: Upside for China?

00:34:31.730 --> 00:34:32.730
QUESTION: Yeah.

00:34:32.730 --> 00:34:38.440
LEVI: Sure. Look, plenty of American companies
would love the support -- at least in some

00:34:38.440 --> 00:34:43.389
ways -- that Chinese companies get from their
government when they're trying to secure investments.

00:34:43.389 --> 00:34:48.150
And when you have a company that has the capacity
to develop a resource, getting access to the

00:34:48.150 --> 00:34:51.470
actual resource is an opportunity for you
to profit.

00:34:51.470 --> 00:34:56.880
So, when it goes well, you can make money.
It contributes to the -- it contributes to

00:34:56.880 --> 00:35:02.730
the state. That's -- that's the kind of way
that China benefits.

00:35:02.730 --> 00:35:08.950
I think there's a more diffuse way that China
benefits, as well. China, as a big consumer

00:35:08.950 --> 00:35:14.570
of resources, benefits when resource supplies
are more abundant.

00:35:14.570 --> 00:35:18.760
And to the extent that Chinese companies can
play a little bit differently -- can take

00:35:18.760 --> 00:35:23.100
a little bit more patience out there; can
take the support of banks that give them low-priced

00:35:23.100 --> 00:35:27.870
capital -- they can actually increase the
world's resource supply.

00:35:27.870 --> 00:35:32.280
Now, that doesn't give China and advantage
over others because that leads to low prices

00:35:32.280 --> 00:35:36.760
or lower prices for everyone. But it does
benefit China as a major consumer.

00:35:36.760 --> 00:35:40.860
And in some cases, the benefit is outsized
in -- if -- in -- in areas where China is

00:35:40.860 --> 00:35:46.100
the dominant consumer of a particular resource,
adding to the supply is primarily a benefit

00:35:46.100 --> 00:35:47.370
for -- for China.

00:35:47.370 --> 00:35:51.150
So, there isn't necessarily a pipeline where
it goes piece by piece right back into China

00:35:51.150 --> 00:35:55.680
and, you know, everything is nicely sewed
up. But China can create conditions where

00:35:55.680 --> 00:35:59.860
-- Chinese producers can create conditions
where Chinese consumers thrive.

00:35:59.860 --> 00:36:02.190
LINDSAY: Sir?

00:36:02.190 --> 00:36:07.080
QUESTION: Eric Stein from Eaton Vance.

00:36:07.080 --> 00:36:11.080
My question is you hear all these horror stories
about when China goes into different countries,

00:36:11.080 --> 00:36:13.990
particularly Africa, that the countries feel
used and abused at the end.

00:36:13.990 --> 00:36:17.740
Are there any countries in your research that
you thought kind of had the best experience

00:36:17.740 --> 00:36:21.790
with China and where other countries could
learn lessons from how best to -- if you're

00:36:21.790 --> 00:36:23.550
a country coming in to deal with China?

00:36:23.550 --> 00:36:27.060
LINDSAY (?): Any -- any upside for the recipients.

00:36:27.060 --> 00:36:31.369
ECONOMY: Sure. I think you can -- you can
find at various points in time, especially

00:36:31.369 --> 00:36:35.940
when the Chinese companies first go in and
when the Chinese are -- are coming in with

00:36:35.940 --> 00:36:40.440
their largesse, right, those trade, aid, investment
deals. There's a lot of excitement about what

00:36:40.440 --> 00:36:42.130
China is going to -- to bring to the table.

00:36:42.130 --> 00:36:47.600
And in -- in some cases, you know, the Chinese
are going in and, you know, going in to invest

00:36:47.600 --> 00:36:52.470
in mines that others have left behind, right?
And so, they're simply not profitable, you

00:36:52.470 --> 00:36:56.119
know, from the perspective of a -- of an American
or an Australian company.

00:36:56.119 --> 00:37:01.040
And -- and so, the Chinese are willing to
go in there -- again, and sometimes, you know,

00:37:01.040 --> 00:37:05.410
with great patience and -- and losing money;
I mean, another thing that we discovered in

00:37:05.410 --> 00:37:10.070
our research is that, you know, something
like 67 percent of -- of Chinese overseas

00:37:10.070 --> 00:37:13.640
investment, not just in natural resources
but across the board, are -- are money losing

00:37:13.640 --> 00:37:20.090
enterprises, which I think is pretty striking;
beats the world average by, you know, 17 percent.

00:37:20.090 --> 00:37:21.090
(LAUGHTER)

00:37:21.090 --> 00:37:26.390
So, that's -- that's pretty significant. So,
I -- I think in terms of one country that's

00:37:26.390 --> 00:37:31.920
benefited -- I mean, look, Mongolia, you know,
certainly has benefited, you know, 90 percent

00:37:31.920 --> 00:37:37.030
of Mongolia's exports -- most of which are
natural resources -- go to China. Fifty percent

00:37:37.030 --> 00:37:41.530
of Chinese -- of foreign direct investment
in Mongolia is from China.

00:37:41.530 --> 00:37:46.320
But does Mongolia feel thankful and grateful
to China? Not so much.

00:37:46.320 --> 00:37:47.320
(LAUGHTER)

00:37:47.320 --> 00:37:51.010
So, I think, you know, there's -- there's
a mix, right? And then some parts of one country

00:37:51.010 --> 00:37:54.330
may feel benefit while other parts certainly
don't.

00:37:54.330 --> 00:37:59.350
LINDSAY: Fair enough. Sir?

00:37:59.350 --> 00:38:05.330
QUESTION: Thank you. Mark Finley with BP.

00:38:05.330 --> 00:38:09.251
You know, the premise of your -- of your book
is kind of go abroad. And you mentioned the

00:38:09.251 --> 00:38:12.790
example of U.S. and other western companies
going abroad early in their histories.

00:38:12.790 --> 00:38:17.140
But the recent history is actually to come
home. And so, you know, the U.S. last year

00:38:17.140 --> 00:38:20.780
had one of the biggest increases in oil production
in the history of the world.

00:38:20.780 --> 00:38:25.720
And my question for both the panelists is
what lessons are Chinese government officials

00:38:25.720 --> 00:38:31.270
and companies taking from the U.S. experience
in that regard, and are they the right lessons?

00:38:31.270 --> 00:38:38.100
LEVI: I don't know that Chinese officials
have taken a macro lesson from the U.S. -- because

00:38:38.100 --> 00:38:41.150
you're talking in particular about what's
happening in -- happening in American oil

00:38:41.150 --> 00:38:42.150
and gas.

00:38:42.150 --> 00:38:47.240
I doubt that there are a lot of Chinese officials
saying, "OK, we need private land rights,

00:38:47.240 --> 00:38:53.680
open financial markets, so on and so forth
and then we'll have our own boom."

00:38:53.680 --> 00:38:59.480
But at a tactical level, there is plenty of
learning going on. And you see this as a pattern

00:38:59.480 --> 00:39:06.760
around the world. Chinese companies go abroad
-- not only to get access to the resource,

00:39:06.760 --> 00:39:12.630
but to partner with others so they can learn
how to develop it -- to get access to technology.

00:39:12.630 --> 00:39:17.500
But in particular, and -- and the U.S. seem
to get access to management expertise to learn

00:39:17.500 --> 00:39:20.330
how you actually plan and develop these sorts
of projects.

00:39:20.330 --> 00:39:23.430
And these aren't the kinds of things where
you can go license a piece of intellectual

00:39:23.430 --> 00:39:26.360
property. You actually have to work alongside.

00:39:26.360 --> 00:39:30.380
And so, you see Chinese companies, you know,
putting up the money for a particular piece

00:39:30.380 --> 00:39:35.690
of acreage along this or that U.S. company
-- so, Sinochem taking a piece of Pioneer's

00:39:35.690 --> 00:39:38.510
Resources in Texas, for example.

00:39:38.510 --> 00:39:42.480
They're not doing that because they've got
an export terminal lined up and it's all going

00:39:42.480 --> 00:39:47.250
to be shipped back to China. It's the expertise
and the understanding of technology that they

00:39:47.250 --> 00:39:51.760
hope to ship back to China so that they can
develop their own resources. I think that's

00:39:51.760 --> 00:39:55.510
the most consequential way that learning is
going on.

00:39:55.510 --> 00:39:57.490
LINDSAY: You want to jump in here, Liz?

00:39:57.490 --> 00:39:58.490
ECONOMY: No, that's fine.

00:39:58.490 --> 00:40:02.120
LINDSAY: I'm going to go -- finally got somebody
in the back. I'm going to go in the back now.

00:40:02.120 --> 00:40:09.680
QUESTION: Thank you. My name Genie Nguyen
from Voice of Vietnamese Americans.

00:40:09.680 --> 00:40:16.970
I have one observation and a question. First,
I congratulate the book. I think the cover

00:40:16.970 --> 00:40:22.730
is very nice. And I particularly like this
picture -- I think it does mean something,

00:40:22.730 --> 00:40:23.730
doesn't it?

00:40:23.730 --> 00:40:30.050
What (ph) make (ph) from the Southeast Asia
and from where all the conflict's happening

00:40:30.050 --> 00:40:33.240
-- this little rock with the flag on it does...

00:40:33.240 --> 00:40:34.240
(LAUGHTER)

00:40:34.240 --> 00:40:35.240
Mean a lot.

00:40:35.240 --> 00:40:39.850
And then the other observation is thank you,
Dr. Levi and Dr. Economy and I -- happens

00:40:39.850 --> 00:40:46.090
to make me think of leverage and economy.
And my question is how do -- how does all

00:40:46.090 --> 00:40:53.050
this -- what is the implications to our U.S.
economy? How do we leverage it?

00:40:53.050 --> 00:40:59.850
And if I'm not mistaken, the U.S. has a lot
to do with China's rise recently, thanks to

00:40:59.850 --> 00:41:08.710
Dr. Kissinger and President Nixon and all
the changes. And I believe that is a -- Dr.

00:41:08.710 --> 00:41:17.730
Kissinger has in a way given out a guiding
hand to help China to move to this stage.

00:41:17.730 --> 00:41:30.100
Now we have all the challenges and we're talking
more about how to embrace China and to better,

00:41:30.100 --> 00:41:36.800
neutral, profitable for everyone globally,
especially from my country, Vietnam.

00:41:36.800 --> 00:41:44.320
So, I'm asking you from your point of view,
what recommendations do you have for the U.S.,

00:41:44.320 --> 00:41:46.810
for ASEAN and for Japan, South Korea...

00:41:46.810 --> 00:41:47.810
(LAUGHTER)

00:41:47.810 --> 00:41:48.810
LEVI: OK, why don't -- why don't we do one...

00:41:48.810 --> 00:41:49.810
QUESTION: And me...

00:41:49.810 --> 00:41:50.810
LINDSAY: Why don't we do one at a time before
we...

00:41:50.810 --> 00:41:51.810
(CROSSTALK)

00:41:51.810 --> 00:41:52.810
QUESTION: Let me just make one -- one -- one
quick (inaudible)...

00:41:52.810 --> 00:41:53.810
(CROSSTALK)

00:41:53.810 --> 00:41:54.810
LEVI: We've got a lot of -- we've got a lot
of recommendations...

00:41:54.810 --> 00:41:56.990
QUESTION: No, no -- the Asia-Pacific rebalancing...

00:41:56.990 --> 00:41:57.990
LINDSAY: OK, OK.

00:41:57.990 --> 00:41:58.990
QUESTION: That's my...

00:41:58.990 --> 00:41:59.990
LINDSAY: You've put an awful lot on the table.
Thank you. But...

00:41:59.990 --> 00:42:03.110
LEVI: We have a lot of recommendations. I
suspect we can each offer up one.

00:42:03.110 --> 00:42:04.110
LINDSAY: OK.

00:42:04.110 --> 00:42:09.950
LEVI: And -- and let me offer up one that
comes from an observation that -- that you

00:42:09.950 --> 00:42:13.000
-- made you ask what you ask, "What impact
does this have for the U.S. economy?"

00:42:13.000 --> 00:42:18.770
The U.S. economy has been hurt by higher resource
prices that have followed it -- not for all

00:42:18.770 --> 00:42:21.240
resources. There are places where the United
States is also a resource exporter.

00:42:21.240 --> 00:42:25.880
But let's say for oil. What can the United
States do? The first thing the United States

00:42:25.880 --> 00:42:29.520
can do is become more efficient in its own
use of resources.

00:42:29.520 --> 00:42:34.990
The most straightforward way, both to remove
pressure on prices but also to protect the

00:42:34.990 --> 00:42:41.410
US. economy, is to be more efficient in our
own use of resources through policy and through

00:42:41.410 --> 00:42:43.880
technology development, which can then be
applied by others.

00:42:43.880 --> 00:42:44.880
LINDSAY: Liz?

00:42:44.880 --> 00:42:50.550
ECONOMY: I guess staying on the economic front,
you know, we are trying to push forward on

00:42:50.550 --> 00:42:53.280
a bilateral investment treaty with China.

00:42:53.280 --> 00:42:58.110
And I think we already have Chinese coming
to look at our land now -- you know, interest

00:42:58.110 --> 00:43:05.930
in from Chinese agricultural firms coming
to buy up or invest in U.S. land. As Mike

00:43:05.930 --> 00:43:10.720
mentioned, we have them, you know, taking
stakes in sort of oil and gas exploration.

00:43:10.720 --> 00:43:15.180
So, I think -- you know, one thing that we
can do is to push forward on this bilateral

00:43:15.180 --> 00:43:21.130
investment treaty while at the same time sort
of ensuring that strategic assets that we

00:43:21.130 --> 00:43:25.750
have, for example, such as rare earths, maintained
within our own purview.

00:43:25.750 --> 00:43:28.680
So I think, you know, we need to learn from
the experience of others. We need to look

00:43:28.680 --> 00:43:33.760
at how China has behaved in terms of its resource
quest -- sort of protect our assets. But at

00:43:33.760 --> 00:43:38.140
the same time, as you say, take advantage
of China's economic rise, its growth and its

00:43:38.140 --> 00:43:40.680
very strong interest in investing in our assets
here.

00:43:40.680 --> 00:43:43.810
LINDSAY: But you have plenty more recommendations
but you have to read the book.

00:43:43.810 --> 00:43:44.810
(LAUGHTER)

00:43:44.810 --> 00:43:45.810
ECONOMY: Yeah.

00:43:45.810 --> 00:43:49.890
LINDSAY: OK, we -- we have time for one last
question. I have to remind everybody before

00:43:49.890 --> 00:43:55.390
I ask -- recognize a questioner that this
meeting has been on the record, OK?

00:43:55.390 --> 00:43:59.400
I'm going to go to the gentleman in the back
-- you get the final question.

00:43:59.400 --> 00:44:04.420
QUESTION: Thank you. My name's Jamey Foger
(ph). I'm in the Navy.

00:44:04.420 --> 00:44:10.420
And I talked to the Chinese navy for our chief
of naval operations. I saw in your book you

00:44:10.420 --> 00:44:14.220
make many references to the South China Sea
-- that's of interest to us.

00:44:14.220 --> 00:44:19.210
And the Chinese navy that we deal with in
the Gulf of Aden is a completely different

00:44:19.210 --> 00:44:23.090
Chinese navy than we deal with in the South
China Sea.

00:44:23.090 --> 00:44:27.310
We try to correct bad behavior in the South
China Sea by being there and being present.

00:44:27.310 --> 00:44:32.780
They don't like it. But we also look for other
ways and we look at other institutions.

00:44:32.780 --> 00:44:38.230
The Chinese have kind of pushed the Philippines
out of the Scarborough Shoal and the Philippines

00:44:38.230 --> 00:44:43.369
has very courageously decided to take that
to the International Tribunal of the Law of

00:44:43.369 --> 00:44:46.030
the Sea. I think they have a pretty good case.
In fact, they've got some very good American

00:44:46.030 --> 00:44:47.880
lawyers working on this.

00:44:47.880 --> 00:44:55.210
If that case goes in favor of the Philippines,
what's your assessment on the -- the change

00:44:55.210 --> 00:45:00.400
or the potential moderation or modulation
of the Chinese and their behavior? Will it

00:45:00.400 --> 00:45:03.400
have any impact or no?

00:45:03.400 --> 00:45:07.810
ECONOMY: I -- I think it'll be interesting
to see what happens. I mean, the Chinese have

00:45:07.810 --> 00:45:14.130
not recognized the Philippines, really -- their
right to go to the Tribunal. So, as near as

00:45:14.130 --> 00:45:20.360
I can tell, they're simply not dealing with
this particular effort on the part of the

00:45:20.360 --> 00:45:21.360
Philippines.

00:45:21.360 --> 00:45:26.840
My guess is it won't have very much effect,
in terms of, you know, moderating Chinese

00:45:26.840 --> 00:45:33.010
behavior. I think the Chinese are continuing
to push -- push out the nine, you know, dash

00:45:33.010 --> 00:45:37.960
line, right? Their claims on the South China
Sea -- they're talking about setting up, you

00:45:37.960 --> 00:45:39.600
know, an air defense identification...

00:45:39.600 --> 00:45:41.090
LINDSAY (?): Identification zone.

00:45:41.090 --> 00:45:46.630
ECONOMY: Thank you. An ADIZ similar to the
one that they just, you know, established

00:45:46.630 --> 00:45:48.540
in the East China Sea.

00:45:48.540 --> 00:45:53.980
I think the Chinese view this as an issue
of sovereignty even more than they do of resources.

00:45:53.980 --> 00:46:01.760
So, I don't anticipate much modulation or
moderation when it comes to the sort of sea

00:46:01.760 --> 00:46:05.220
claims on the -- regional sea claims on the
part of the Chinese.

00:46:05.220 --> 00:46:07.340
LINDSAY: So, you see the Chinese dismissing
them?

00:46:07.340 --> 00:46:08.810
ECONOMY: I see them dismissing them.

00:46:08.810 --> 00:46:10.820
LINDSAY: You want to jump in here, Michael?

00:46:10.820 --> 00:46:16.351
LEVI: I think that builds pretty clearly on
the analysis that we did in the book. I think

00:46:16.351 --> 00:46:19.690
you identify a nice dichotomy.

00:46:19.690 --> 00:46:26.011
And we look at the Gulf of Aden deployment
and that's where China's learning. But we

00:46:26.011 --> 00:46:28.320
also look at the South and East China Seas.

00:46:28.320 --> 00:46:33.040
And Liz emphasized this core point -- this
is not just about resources. And it's not

00:46:33.040 --> 00:46:37.630
even just about freedom of navigation in the
sea lanes, which has a strong connection to

00:46:37.630 --> 00:46:44.720
resources. It's about nationalism and sovereignty
and that's an extraordinarily powerful driver.

00:46:44.720 --> 00:46:51.930
LINDSAY: I think you can see from the very
sophisticated and nuanced explanations that

00:46:51.930 --> 00:46:58.300
Liz and Michael have offered up tonight why
"By All Means Necessary" is a terrific book

00:46:58.300 --> 00:47:03.620
and why we are very, very proud to have them
as members of the studies program.

00:47:03.620 --> 00:47:05.520
So, please join me in thanking Liz and Michael.

00:47:05.520 --> 00:47:06.520
(APPLAUSE)

00:47:06.520 --> 00:47:06.521
END

